{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Repair the migration and upgrade the catalog from v1 to v2 while retaining existing product IDs and descriptions and adding price_cents as a non-null, nonnegative field initialized to zero.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The original catalog contained products (1, Paper) and (2, Tape) at version 1. The repaired migration produced version 2 with the same IDs and descriptions, price_cents=0 for both rows, a NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 CHECK constraint, and an intact database. The negative-value rejection was directly tested.","evidence_ids":["cmd-98fd20822dc14635","cmd-5411dcac5be147ac","cmd-e8a5e785b1b344f4","cmd-1df02ba18a57432a","cmd-68eb705c04344ef3"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Make the upgrade transactional so schema_version advances only after the whole upgrade succeeds and a failed upgrade leaves the v1 database usable.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A forced failure during the version update rolled back the preceding column addition. The resulting test database remained at version 1 with both original rows, the original two-column products schema, and a successful integrity check.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2a30c029b4a54702"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Make the migration safe to repeat.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired migration completed twice on an isolated v1 copy and twice on the production catalog without duplicate schema changes or data damage. It also completed successfully again before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-59c360b657f3493b","cmd-e8a5e785b1b344f4","cmd-5ea915dd162846df","cmd-68eb705c04344ef3"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The implementation uses the installed Python 2.4/PySQLite stack. CentOS 5.11 and kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 were observed before and after the work and restart, with the fixed lifecycle platform snapshot also unchanged.","evidence_ids":["